===Etymology 1===
Unclear. ''{{w|Shuowen}}'' suggests that the word arises from the startling size of the taro, leading Kai Xu to connect it to {{och-l|吁|id=1}}, which is the sound that one makes when one is startled. However, this seems to be folk etymology.

Compare {{cog|hmx-pro|*wouH||taro}}, {{cog|my|ဝ||elephant foot yam}}, {{cog|bo|གྲོ་མ|'''གྲོ'''་མ|{{taxfmt|Argentina anserina|species}} (syn. {{taxfmt|Potentilla anserina|species}}), a plant with small edible tubers}}. There are various theories on how all these words are related:
* {{zh-ref|Schuessler (2007)}} considers it to be an {{w|Mainland Southeast Asian linguistic area|areal}} word, comparing it to the {{cog|hmx|-}} and {{cog|my|-}} words. {{zh-ref|Schuessler (2015)}} does not consider the {{cog|bo|-}} word to be cognate.
* {{zh-ref|Blench (2012)}} suggests that the Chinese word is borrowed from {{bor|zh|hmx-pro|-}} and that the {{cog|my|-}} word may be a late loan from {{cog|och|-}}.
* STEDT reconstructs {{cog|sit-pro|*g/s-rwa||taro; yam; tuber}}, whence the {{cog|bo|-}} word. This etymon is regarded as allofamically related this word and {{och-l|薯}}.
* {{zh-ref|Gong Hwang-cherng (2002)}} and {{zh-ref|Baxter and Sagart (2017)}} also suggest that this word is related to the {{cog|bo|-}} word.

