{{zh-forms}}
It is commonly believed in Southwest China and Hubei that these meanings are extended from the sense "sweet potato" ({{zh-ref|Sun, 2014}}). However, this is almost certainly a folk etymology, because its synonymous variants are widely used in different Chinese topolects (e.g. {{zh-l|杓|sháo}} in northern Mandarin and {{zh-l|痟|siáu}} in Hokkien) while the sense "sweet potato" is constricted to Southwestern Mandarin, and the earliest attestation of such forms predates the voyages of Christopher Columbus. {{zh-ref|Yang (2013)}} proposes that the derogatory senses of {{lang|zh|苕}} are derived from {{zh-l|屌|penis}}.

